      {
        "id": 215914,
        "name": "Gus_Smedstad",
        "username": "Gus_Smedstad",
        "avatar_template": "/letter_avatar_proxy/v4/letter/g/e8c25b/{size}.png",
        "created_at": "2018-04-04T12:54:17.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Mint.</p><p>Easier than I thought it would be. Infinitor took a lot of hard knocks early, which made me think I’d have to deal with the flip, but after that I had little trouble keeping him alive. This, despite stupid things like forgetting again that Into The Stratosphere doesn’t work on indestructible manifestations.</p><p>Early on the main threat to Infinitor was the Lambent Reapers, but once Captain Cosmic put a few constructs in play, they started chasing those.</p><p>Double Aggression Modulators on the Thorian Military meant I had no real need to do damage, since the Thorians were blasting away at them for 6 damage per turn. Mostly my heroes sat idle while waiting for Infinitor to play one of his “1 damage to everything” cards, which took a while.</p><p>Otherworldly Resilience and Energy Bracer meant Tempest could tank any damage redirected away from the Abject Refugees. I played Thorian Monolith a couple of times to reduce damage from manifestations, but eventually Captain Cosmic got the Energy Crest out, which neutered most of the manifestations.</p><p>The environment was the main threat to Infinitor. It helped that a couple of Syphons on Tempest meant he could retaliate immediately with Grevious Hailstorm, which sometimes killed off the environment card before it could do anything else.&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 9,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:47:28.500Z",
